WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.332 --> 00:00:03.107
But compared to Eddy's, I think it's a little bit more closed.

00:00:03.107 --> 00:00:05.366
But it's a lot sweeter, so I'm hoping...

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:07.383
I'm playing it, I don't know how long it normally takes,

00:00:07.383 --> 00:00:08.906
for it to kind of start opening up.

00:00:08.906 --> 00:00:11.120
Do you know&nbsp;how to play in a violin?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.500
Oh, there's a way?

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.540
- Yeah, I did not know that, I was...
- Yeah, my way!
- Wait what?

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.660
- I have a way.
- Oh!

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.242
So sometimes I'll go to violin&nbsp;shops and just play instruments

00:00:20.242 --> 00:00:22.182
that haven't been played a lot,

00:00:22.182 --> 00:00:24.568
just as like a favor to a friend&nbsp;or whatever,

00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:26.580
if they have a new violin that they just made,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.039
or if it's a shop where I'm just trying&nbsp;instruments for fun,

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:34.495
I'll like, pay it forward by then playing on something

00:00:34.495 --> 00:00:38.072
that hasn't had enough&nbsp;hours to really show itself yet.

00:00:38.072 --> 00:00:39.654
The way I do it is I take—

00:00:39.654 --> 00:00:41.160
I give it like an hour,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.833
and just in the&nbsp;course of the hour, I play as loud as possible,

00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:49.614
- Oh, right.
- for about a minute or so at a time,

00:00:49.614 --> 00:00:51.592
and then I play&nbsp;close to the bridge,

00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:56.033
and I really try to make it like ugly, edgy, loud.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:59.842
I just find the edge&nbsp;of the sound, and then I play really quietly,

00:00:59.842 --> 00:01:02.487
and I find the edge of where the tone is.

00:01:02.487 --> 00:01:05.700
It's&nbsp;like a rubber band, like you keep stretching it,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.229
and letting it relax.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:08.848
Stretch it, let it relax.

00:01:08.848 --> 00:01:11.304
And&nbsp;then whatever it's not comfortable doing,

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:16.722
you just do that magnified, for like five, ten&nbsp;minutes.

00:01:16.722 --> 00:01:18.900
So like if it doesn't wanna...

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.462
...speak with harmonics,

00:01:20.462 --> 00:01:23.859
it could be that the wood is&nbsp;like, tight in that area,

00:01:23.859 --> 00:01:25.620
or maybe it could be that...

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.094
...you're not comfortable with the way

00:01:28.094 --> 00:01:29.777
you need&nbsp;to draw out the sound,

00:01:29.777 --> 00:01:31.621
or maybe the resonance patterns

00:01:31.621 --> 00:01:33.199
haven't established themselves yet,

00:01:33.199 --> 00:01:36.218
so you have a chance to really shape the new instruments,

00:01:36.218 --> 00:01:37.940
and shape how they vibrate.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.387
That's&nbsp;why when I...when I'm playing a new instrument,

00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:41.390
and I'm thinking,

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.726
"There's something that's&nbsp;not happening right here,"

00:01:43.726 --> 00:01:48.353
I really try to dig...into it, and find that, and draw it out.

00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:51.353
And&nbsp;remarkably it actually does happen,

00:01:51.353 --> 00:01:53.643
like in the course of an hour, a violin...

00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:56.608
A new violin&nbsp;will change significantly with

00:01:56.608 --> 00:01:59.383
being pushed to its limits.
